    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Colchis exported gold, iron, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and honey mostly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a>, and Greek and Roman sources say between 70 and 300 languages were spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dioscurias">Dioscurias</a> alone.</li><li>Sarduri II of Urartu recorded a victory over Qulḫa in the mid-8th century BCE, and Askold Ivanchik holds that Qulḫa flourished as an independent state in the second half of that century.</li><li>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a> the southern tribes formed the 19th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> while the northern tribes sent 100 girls and 100 boys to the Persian court every five years.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The oldest 'Colchian' coins, dated to the mid-6th century BCE and long the primary evidence for a Colchian state, have on reassessment turned out to be the production of an Achaemenid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

