    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Colchis was the Greek name for the Georgian polity of Egrisi on the eastern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, in what is now western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> with Abkhazia and the neighbouring coasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Its people, the Colchians, are generally believed to have been early Zan-speaking tribes, ancestors of the Laz and Mingrelians, and Cyril Toumanoff called Colchis 'not a proto-Georgian, but a Georgian (West Georgian) kingdom', the earliest Georgian formation. The land is a humid plain between the Greater and Lesser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, closed on the east by the Likhi Range, where temperate rainforest once covered the lowlands. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> Colchian culture, related to the Koban culture, grew up there, and by the end of the second millennium BCE urbanisation was well advanced in parts of the country; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> brought skill in smelting and casting metals and sophisticated farming implements.</p><p>The name is thought to derive from the Urartian Qulḫa. Giorgi Melikishvili and Donald Rayfield read Colchis into Assyrian inscriptions: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tukulti-ninurta-i">Tukulti-Ninurta I</a> (1243–1207 BCE) defeated forty kings of Nairi and of 'the countries on the shores of the Upper Sea', and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-i">Tiglath-Pileser I</a> (1114–1076 BCE) called himself conqueror of Nairi 'from Gilḫi to the Upper Sea', Gilḫi being read as Kilḫi, Colchis. In the mid-8th century BCE Sarduri II of Urartu inscribed a victory over Qulḫa, whose exact location is disputed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nokalakevi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nokalakevi.jpg" alt="Nokalakevi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nokalakevi">Nokalakevi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nokalakevi99.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lika (ლიკა), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Greek writers described Colchis as a land rich in gold, iron, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and honey, exporting its resources mostly to the Hellenic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>, and in myth as a fabulously wealthy country on the periphery of the heroic world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">Millet</a> was the staple crop; wheat grew in some regions and was also imported by sea, and local wines were drunk alongside wines brought from overseas. The Colchian plain gave ample grazing for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and horses, the name of Phasis being associated with fine horses, and its wetlands held waterfowl: Colchian pheasants were exported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where moralists condemned them as a symbol of excess. Colchis provided slaves as tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, and Colchian slaves are attested in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><p>The country's towns lay along the shore and the rivers: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dioscurias">Dioscurias</a>, later the Roman Sebastopolis and now Sukhumi, Phasis at the mouth of the river of that name, the Rioni, Pityus (Pitsunda), Apsaros (Gonio), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shorapani">Sarapana</a> (Shorapani), Surium (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vani">Vani</a>), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nokalakevi">Archaeopolis</a> (Nokalakevi) and Cyta or Aia (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a>), the traditional birthplace of Medea. The population was diverse: Greek and Roman sources say that between 70 and 300 languages were spoken in Dioscurias alone. The influence of the Achaemenid Empire, with its thriving commerce and wide economic ties, accelerated the socio-economic development of the Colchian land. Greek was widespread, and decrees were even issued in that language.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus.jpg" alt="Mithridates VI of Pontus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mithridates_VI_Louvre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Colchis fought its wars against the great powers to the south and the horsemen from the north. The polity of Kilkhi or Qulḫa warred first against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-i">Tiglath-Pileser I</a> and then against Urartu under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argishti-i">Argishti I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menua">Menua</a>, and Sarduri II of Urartu claimed a victory over it in the mid-8th century BCE. From that century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a> migrating from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> into the Pontic steppes invaded Colchis, and the Scythians made a base on the former territory of the kingdom from which they raided and campaigned in Media and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>; Askold Ivanchik judges that Qulḫa, an independent flourishing state in the second half of the 8th century BCE, hardly survived the century's end.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a> the southern tribes, the Macrones, Moschi and Marres, were incorporated into the 19th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a>, while the northern tribes submitted 'voluntarily' and sent the Persian court 100 girls and 100 boys every five years. In 400 BCE, shortly after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-thousand">Ten Thousand</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trapezus</a>, the Colchians fought them and were decisively defeated. Later the Colchians appear to have thrown off Achaemenid authority and formed an independent state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a> quelled an uprising in the region in 83 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> occupied Colchis after Mithridates' defeat in 65 BCE, capturing the local chief Olthaces.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aeschylus.jpg" alt="Aeschylus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herma_of_Aeschylus,_Klas08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Little is known of the rulers of Colchis. Pliny says the Colchians were governed by their own kings in the earliest ages, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> knew the mythical Aeëtes, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helios">Helios</a> and father of Medea, with his city Aea or Aia, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a> first used Kolchis for a region. After the Achaemenid period the Colchians formed an independent state that, according to Ronald Suny, was federated to Kartli-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> in the east; its kings ruled through skeptoukhoi, royal governors who received a staff from the king, and by David Braund's reading of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> the native dynasty continued to rule despite the country's fragmentation into skeptoukhies. The oldest 'Colchian' coins, from the mid-6th century BCE, long served as the primary evidence for a Colchian state, but reassessment shows them to be the production of an Achaemenid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a>.</p><p>Classical sources bordered Colchis with Pontus to the south-west, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> to the west, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-caucasus">Greater Caucasus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarmatia">Sarmatia</a> to the north, Iberia to the east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> to the south. In 83 BCE Mithridates VI gave the country to his son Mithridates, who was executed on suspicion of plotting against him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> installed Aristarchus as dynast (63–47 BCE); under Polemon I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythodoris">Pythodorida</a> of Pontus and Polemon II Colchis belonged to Pontus until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> induced Polemon to abdicate and the land was absorbed into the province of Galatia in 63 and Cappadocia in 81.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jason"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jason.jpg" alt="Jason" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jas%C3%A3o_e_o_Velo_de_ouro_-_Bertel_Thorvaldsen_-_1803.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bertel Thorvaldsen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Colchis is reckoned in modern scholarship 'the earliest Georgian formation', which with the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> contributed to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and the Georgian nation; Ronald Grigor Suny stresses the Colchians' part in the ethnogenesis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>, and Svante Cornell dates proto-Georgian statehood to the kingdom of Colchis in the 6th century BCE. The name Colchians became the Greco-Roman collective term for the early Kartvelian tribes of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast, and in a later era it was joined to the name Lazi: by the 1st century BCE the kingdom of Lazica had formed in the region, known as Egrisi when it became a Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> in 66 BCE after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>'s campaign.</p><p>To the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> Colchis was Aea, from the 5th century BCE onwards the home of Aeëtes, Medea and the Golden Fleece, the goal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argonauts">Argonauts</a>; here Prometheus was chained to a mountain for revealing fire to humanity, and here some placed the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazons">Amazons</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, who is thought never to have gone there, imagined the Colchians dark-skinned and woolly-haired, called them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> and said they practised circumcision as descendants of the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> Sesostris, claims modern historians have widely rejected. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, by traditional accounts, was first preached in Colchis by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-the-apostle">Andrew the Apostle</a>, Simon the Zealot and Matthias; a change in burial patterns in the 3rd century may show its influence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/arrian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arrian.jpg" alt="Arrian" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arrian7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WikiSysop, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Colchian state did not fall at once; it was absorbed. After it threw off Achaemenid authority it ruled through its skeptoukhoi, but in 83 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a> quelled an uprising in the region and gave Colchis to his son Mithridates, soon executed on suspicion of plotting against his father. On Mithridates' defeat in 65 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> occupied the country, captured the chief Olthaces and installed Aristarchus as dynast from 63 to 47 BCE. Under Polemon I Colchis was part of Pontus and the Bosporan Kingdom; his widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythodoris">Pythodorida</a> kept it, and her son Polemon II was induced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> to abdicate, after which Pontus and Colchis were incorporated into the province of Galatia in 63 and Cappadocia in 81.</p><p>Phasis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dioscurias">Dioscurias</a> and the other Greek settlements of the coast never fully recovered from the wars of 60–40 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> became the economic and political centre of the region. Roman rule was relatively loose: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> held the sea forts, mountain tribes such as the Svaneti and Heniochi raided the lowlands and ran their own kingdoms with nominal homage, and the uprising of 69 under Anicetus failed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>, sent by Hadrian, found the Laz in control of most of coastal Colchis. Out of this the kingdom of Lazica, or Egrisi, emerged, the heir of Colchis in the Georgian tradition, while the name Colchis lived on in Greek and Roman geography and in the legend of Medea.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Colchis, the Georgian Egrisi on the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, was known to Urartu as Qulḫa and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as the land of the Golden Fleece; a state in the 8th century BCE and again from the 6th, tributary to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, it ruled through royal governors until Pontus and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> absorbed it in the 1st century BCE.</em></p>

